## Do You Need to Claim a Trump Account to Get the SpaceX Share?

No official source states that claiming a Trump Account is required to receive the SpaceX share, nor does any source state that claiming makes an account active. [SpaceX](https://www.spacex.com/) announced in its [primary investor release](https://ir.spacex.com/updates/releases-details/2026/SpaceX-to-Invest-in-the-Next-Generation-of-Americans-2026-r_LYQ5cH94/default.aspx) that it will grant one newly issued Class A share to every currently active Trump Account, but neither SpaceX nor Treasury guidance defines what qualifies an account as active. While auto-enrolled children can receive philanthropic stock contributions without a claimed account, you should still consider completing the claim process on [trumpaccounts.gov](https://trumpaccounts.gov) to secure the one-time $1,000 Treasury deposit for eligible children born between 2025 and 2028, unlock family and employer contributions up to $5,000 per year, and take legal control of your child's investments. 

If you decide to claim the account now, Treasury outlines six steps on [trumpaccounts.gov](https://trumpaccounts.gov) or through our [guide on how to claim a Trump Account](/guides/how-to-claim-a-trump-account/):

1. Download the official Trump Accounts app from the App Store or Google Play.
2. Verify your identity through the application interface.
3. Confirm your legal relationship to the child.
4. Review the child's recorded personal and enrollment information.
5. Accept the official account terms and conditions.
6. Optionally claim the one-time $1,000 Treasury contribution if your child meets the birth-year eligibility criteria.

### What Is Confirmed and What Is Not

| Item | Status | Source and date |
| --- | --- | --- |
| SpaceX commitment to every currently active Trump Account | Confirmed: one newly issued Class A share per active account | SpaceX primary release, November 26, 2026 |
| Musk's matching share | Confirmed: personal match of one share per account | SpaceX primary release, November 26, 2026 |
| Musk's share transfer | Expected June 2027 following personal lockup expiration | [CNBC](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/11/25/spacex-musk-trump-account-stock.html) citing [Musk's post on X](https://x.com/elonmusk), November 26, 2026 |
| Shotwell's earlier gift | Over 2 million shares expected to reach recipients in December | CNBC, November 26, 2026 |
| SpaceX's own share transfer date | Not announced | SpaceX primary release, November 26, 2026 |
| Definition of active | Not defined by SpaceX or Treasury | SpaceX primary release and CNBC, November 26, 2026 |
| Claim requirement for the SpaceX share | Not stated by any source | SpaceX primary release and CNBC, November 26, 2026 |
| Sale restriction | Shares cannot be sold until the earlier of five years or the end of the year the child turns 17 | CNBC, citing Treasury rules, November 26, 2026 |

Our answer on whether you must claim the account would change if SpaceX publishes clear eligibility criteria defining an active account, or if the Treasury Department releases administrative guidance establishing that a parent claim is mandatory to accept corporate equity donations. Until either party issues an official update clarifying distribution mechanics, claiming remains necessary to manage the cash balance and deposit annual contributions, but it remains unconfirmed as a condition for receiving the SpaceX share.